{
  "_comment": "esm-spec §9.5.5 load-time table checks and their pinned order: the cases JSON cannot express as a `.esm` document. Each case is one function table (id `T`) and one `table_lookup` node referencing it. A harness builds the table IN MEMORY from `table`, writing each string \"NaN\", \"Inf\" or \"-Inf\" as that binary64 value (JSON cannot carry them), and runs it through the SAME load-time table check that the binding's load / validate() runs on every `function_tables` entry (ruling Q-106); the axes with fewer than 2 values are here because the schema rejects them (`minItems: 2`). The harness asserts the diagnostic code (`expected_error_code`; null means the table passes and the lookup lowers). Cases that break two rules break two ADJACENT rules of the order; `breaks` lists the rules each case breaks, first the one to report. The schema-valid cases are ordinary documents under tests/invalid/ (function_table_*.esm, pinned `resolver_only` with `resolver_error_code` in tests/invalid/expected_errors.json). A non-finite value (NaN, +Inf or -Inf) in an axis is `table_axis_nan` and in `data` is `table_data_nan` (ruling Q-109). A binding whose table data cannot hold a non-finite value at all (its data type has none) may skip a case whose `data` holds one, and says so in its harness.",
  "spec": "esm-spec §9.5.5 (Order of the table checks); formal: Esm.Closed.Tables.validateTable",
  "table_id": "T",
  "order": [
    "table_axis_duplicate_name",
    "table_outputs_duplicate_name",
    "table_interpolation_axes_mismatch",
    "table_axis_nan (any non-finite axis value)",
    "table_axis_non_monotonic",
    "table_axis_too_short",
    "table_outputs_length_mismatch",
    "table_data_shape_mismatch (nesting of data)",
    "table_data_nan (any non-finite data leaf)",
    "table_data_shape_mismatch (shape assertion)"
  ],
  "cases": [
    {
      "name": "control_valid",
      "description": "A well-formed 1-axis linear table. It lowers; no code. Proves the harness reaches the lowering with a table it built.",
      "breaks": [],
      "table": {
        "axes": [
          {
            "name": "x",
            "values": [
              0,
              1,
              2
            ]
          }
        ],
        "interpolation": "linear",
        "data": [
          10,
          20,
          30
        ]
      },
      "lookup": {
        "op": "table_lookup",
        "table": "T",
        "axes": {
          "x": 0.5
        },
        "args": []
      },
      "expected_error_code": null
    },
    {
